          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
            is not
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed"} cartesian closed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinite-dimensional+manifolds"} infinite-dimensional manifolds
            are allowed. However, that does not mean that
            %em no
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+spaces"} mapping spaces
            between certain smooth manifolds can be given the structure of a smooth manifold. This is true when the source is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+topological+space"} compact
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/loop+spaces"} loop spaces
          %p
            The method of proving this depends mostly on the structure of the target and only minimally on that of the source. It is not hard to generalise it to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifolds+with+boundary"} manifolds with boundary
            (to get, for example,
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+spaces"} path spaces
          #mfdconj.num_theorem
            %h6#conjecture Conjecture
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%B6licher+space"} Frölicher space
              whose
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvaceous+topology"} curvaceous topology
              is
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequentially+compact"} sequentially compact
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              be a smooth manifold that admits a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+addition"} local addition
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              is a smooth manifold.
          %h2#ConstructionOfSmoothManifoldStructureOnMappingSpace Construction of smooth manifold structure on mapping space
          %blockquote
            %p
              The following needs attention. For a more recent version see (
              %a{:href => "#Stacey"}> Stacey
              ).
          %h3#background_and_remarks Background and Remarks
          %p The question discussed here can be viewed as the counterpoint to the oft-heard maxim (attributed to Grothendieck):
          %blockquote
            %p It is better to work in a nice category with nasty objects than in a nasty category with nice objects.
          %p
            Smooth manifolds are an example of “nice objects in a nasty category”; for example, one can rarely take subobjects or quotients. The standard procedure at this point is to embed the nasty category in some larger, nicer category and work there. In the case of smooth manifolds, this has led to all of the categories that are listed at
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+smooth+space"} generalized smooth space
          %p One can now go on to study this enlarged category, and investigate how much of what is known about the original category extends to the larger one. In this line, the original category is viewed mainly as a source of ideas. An alternative approach, and that taken here, is to view the original category as being a subcategory of “special objects” inside the larger one.
          %p One can make an analogy with the real and complex numbers. Many aspects of the study of real numbers become much easier and clearer when extended to the complex numbers. At this point, one has a choice: one can simply study the complex numbers or one can use the complex numbers as a tool to study the real ones.
          %p
            Thus, to adapt a saying of Hadamard (
            %a{:href => "Jacques+Hadamard#TheShortestPath"}> here
            ), we could introduce our own maxim:
          %blockquote
            %p The shortest distance between two truths about nice objects often lies in a nice category.
          %p
            Having mentioned the plethora of extensions of the category of smooth manifolds, we should comment on our choice of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%B6licher+spaces"} Frölicher spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+spaces"} diffeological spaces
            then we can split the question “Is the diffeological space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            a smooth manifold?” into “Is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            a Frölicher space?” and “Is the resulting Frölicher space a manifold?”. Moreover, as we are interested in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi N
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(N,M)
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            a smooth manifold (and thus a Frölicher space), then if we are working with one of the “maps in” approaches, we can replace the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi N
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(N,M)
            by its “Frölicherification” without changing the set. Thus the key piece of the puzzle is to study
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi N
                  %mo ,
                  %mi M
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(N,M)
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            a Frölicher space and the rest will follow by applying “general nonsense”.
          %p
            Another remark worth saying is that the conjecture stated is not the most general statement that could be considered. It is simple to extend this conjecture to a relative version whereby
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi M
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
            is equipped with a family of submanifolds and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            with a family of subsets and the maps are constrained to take the subsets to the corresponding submanifolds.
          %p
            Finally, let us note that the main results about the linear model spaces are recorded on the page
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+mapping+spaces"} linear mapping spaces
