                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Psi_{2 1}
              is a diffeomorphism.
          %h3#conclusion Conclusion
          %p In conclusion we have
          #cordiff.num_corollary
            %h6#corollary Corollary
            %p
              The mapping space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi N
                    %mo ,
                    %mi M
                    %mo ;
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi Q
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi P
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(N,M;\{Q_i\},\{P_i\})
              of def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TheMappingSpace"}
              equipped with charts as discussed
              %a{:href => "#Charts"} above
              is a smooth manifold.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#RelationBetweenDiffeologicalAndFrechetStructure Relation between diffeological and Fréchet manifold structure
          %p
            Since smooth manifolds form a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+spaces"} diffeological spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(X,Y)
            between two manifolds always exists canonically as a diffeological space:
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ∈
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \in
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CartSp"} CartSp
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi diff
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(X,Y)_{diff}
              over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+function"} smooth function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \times X \to Y
              (hence a
              = succeed "-parameterized" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to Y
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+manifold"} compact manifold
            then there is also the structure of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9chet+manifold"} Fréchet manifold
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Y
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi Fr
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(X,Y)_{Fr}
            on the mapping space. We discuss that and how these two smooth structures coincide.
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p Define a functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ι
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi FrechetManifolds
                    %mo →
                    %mi DiffeologicalSpaces
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\iota \colon FrechetManifolds \to DiffeologicalSpaces
            %p
              in the evident way by taking for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9chet+manifold"} Fréchet manifold
              for any
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ∈
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CartSp"} CartSp
              the set of
              = succeed "-plots" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ι
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \iota(X)
              to be the set of smooth functions
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \to X
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              The functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ι
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi FrechetManifolds
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi DiffeologicalSpaces
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \iota \colon FrechetManifolds \hookrightarrow DiffeologicalSpaces
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+and+faithful+functor"} full and faithful functor
          %p
            This appears as (
            %a{:href => "#Losik"}> Losik, theorem 3.1.1
            ).
          %p
            Since moreover diffeological spaces are fully faithful in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+sets"} smooth sets
            (them being precisely the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concrete+objects"} concrete objects
            in smooth sets), this implies in particular that Frechet manifolds are fully faithful in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+sets"} smooth sets
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi FrechetManifolds
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi DiffeologicalSpaces
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi SmoothSets
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  FrechetManifolds
                  \\hookrightarrow
                  DiffeologicalSpaces
                  \\hookrightarrow
                  SmoothSets
                  \\,.
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi SMoothManifold
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X, Y \in SMoothManifold
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+manifold"} compact manifold
            %p
              Then under this embedding, the diffeological mapping space structure
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi diff
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(X,Y)_{diff}
              on the mapping space coincides with the Fréchet manifold structure
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Y
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi Fr
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(X,Y)_{Fr}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ι
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi Fr
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi diff
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\iota(C^\infty(X,Y)_{Fr})
                    \\simeq
                    C^\infty(X,Y)_{diff}
                    \\,.
          %p
            This appears as (
            %a{:href => "#Waldorf"}> Waldorf, lemma A.1.7
            ).
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li#Losik
              %p
                M. V. Losik,
                %em Categorical Differential Geometry
                Cah. Topol. Géom. Différ. Catég., 35(4):274–290, 1994.
            %li#Waldorf
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Konrad+Waldorf"}> Konrad Waldorf
                ,
                %em Transgression to Loop Spaces and its Inverse I
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0911.3212"}> arXiv:0911.3212
                )
            %li#Stacey
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrew+Stacey"}> Andrew Stacey
                ,
                %em Yet more smooth mapping spaces and their smoothly local properties
                arXiv:
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1301.5493"} 1301.5493
          %p
            Generalising the Lie group structure on the diffeomorphisms of a manifold, the case of non-compact
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifolds"} orbifolds
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Schmeding"}> Alexander Schmeding
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The diffeomorphism group of a non-compact orbifold
              %strong 507
              (2015), DOI:
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.4064/dm507-0-1"} 10.4064/dm507-0-1
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://digital.ub.uni-paderborn.de/ubpb/urn/urn:nbn:de:hbz:466:2-12166"} nbn-resolving.de/urn:nbn:de:hbz:466:2-12166
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1301.5551"} 1301.5551
          %p
            The generalization to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+stacks"} mapping stacks
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differentiable+stacks"} differentiable stacks
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li#RobertsVozzo16
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Michael+Roberts"}> David Michael Roberts
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Raymond+Vozzo"} Raymond Vozzo
              = succeed "," do
                %em The smooth Hom-stack of an orbifold
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-72299-3_3"} 10.1007/978-3-319-72299-3_3
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.05904"} arXiv:1610.05904
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "https://www.matrix-inst.org.au/2016-matrix-annals/"} MATRIX hosted version
